 "section_summary": "This section contains the concluding portions of an initial copy of U.S. Embassy Tokyo's monthly report for October 1940 (Report No. 1669) together with its appended monthly schedules of telegrams and despatches, followed by the transmittal Despatch No. 5160 (dated November 20, 1940) and the opening sixteen pages of a second copy of the same report. The report details worsening Japanese-American relations in the wake of the Tripartite Pact, U.S. embargoes on scrap iron, the Department of State's recommendation for American nationals to evacuate the Far East, and diplomatic protests regarding Japanese bombing raids and trade restrictions in China. It also surveys Japan's regional diplomacy and military posture—including the reopening and aerial bombing of the Burma Road, trade and oil negotiations in the Netherlands East Indies, economic missions to French Indochina, and rapprochement with the Soviet Union—alongside domestic political regimentation under Prince Konoye's New National Structure and the Imperial Rule Assistance Association.",
 "subfiles": [
  {
   "about": "Continuation of Embassy Tokyo's monthly report on political, economic, diplomatic, and military conditions in Japan during October 1940, accompanied by schedules of telegrams and despatches sent to the Department.",
