   "start_page": 138,
   "summary": "This report details the friction in Japanese-American relations following the Tripartite Alliance, addressing Foreign Minister Matsuoka's peace assertions, U.S. protests over Japanese bombings and commercial restrictions in China, and Japan's formal notice terminating the 1911 North Pacific fur seals convention. It reviews foreign relations including Britain's reopening of the Burma Road and subsequent Japanese air attacks, Japanese military movements in China and French Indochina, trade negotiations in Batavia for East Indies petroleum, and diplomatic overtures toward a Soviet-Japanese non-aggression pact. Domestically, it covers the inauguration of the Imperial Rule Assistance Association, accusations of communism directed against Prince Konoye's New National Structure, extensive commodity rationing, electric power and food shortages, and the forced sale of the American-owned Japan Advertiser. The subfile concludes with complete chronological lists of all telegrams (Nos. 924–1083) and despatches (Nos. 5026–5105) sent by the Embassy to the Department of State during October 1940.",
   "title": "Report of Conditions in Japan During the Month of October, 1940 (Report No. 1669)",
   "to": "Department of State"
  },
  {
   "about": "Despatch No. 5160 from Ambassador Joseph C. Grew transmitting Report No. 1669 on conditions in Japan for October 1940, including index and report text through page 16.",
