   ],
   "start_page": 184,
   "summary": "Ambassador Joseph C. Grew transmits to the Secretary of State the Embassy's confidential monthly report (Report No. 1669) reviewing political, diplomatic, and economic developments in Japan for October 1940, with distribution indicated to various Asian missions and naval commands. The enclosed report provides an index and summary before opening Section I on Japanese-American relations, emphasizing the State Department's advice encouraging American citizens to withdraw from the Far East. It recounts Ambassador Grew's meetings with Foreign Minister Matsuoka and Vice Minister Ohashi concerning the defensive claims and aggressive implications of the Tripartite Pact, Japan's abrogation of the 1911 fur seals convention, and numerous formal representations protesting Japanese bombings and trade curbs in China. The section breaks off in the initial paragraphs of Section II discussing Great Britain's announcement regarding the reopening of the Burma Road.",
   "title": "Despatch No. 5160, Transmitting Report of Conditions in Japan During the Month of October 1940",
   "to": "Secretary of State"
  }
